 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Food preservation is treating food so that it does not go bad, by stopping the growth of microorganisms (bacteria, moulds) and the action of enzymes.\nMicroorganisms need water, warmth, air and food. Methods remove one of these:\n• drying (sun-drying fish, cocoa, maize, cassava) removes water;\n• smoking (fish, meat) dries and adds chemicals that kill germs;\n• salting and sugaring (salted fish, jam) draw out water;\n• cooling and freezing (fridge, freezer) slow germs;\n• heating: boiling, pasteurising (milk), canning (sealed tins);\n• adding preservatives such as vinegar (pickling).\nImportance: less waste, food available all year, food can be carried far, and it prevents food poisoning.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "f": "fish"
  },
  "problem": "How does smoking preserve {f}?",
  "steps": [
   "Smoke and heat dry the {f}, removing the water that germs need.",
   "The smoke also contains chemicals that kill germs."
  ],
  "answer": "It removes water and kills germs.",
  "checks": []
 },
